WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.200
Sitting at your desk looking at your monitor&nbsp;
during the day, there’s really nothing out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.940
of the ordinary. But if the sun sets, or I&nbsp;
just cut the lights, and the room darkens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:13.860
you get this characteristic glow from the&nbsp;
monitor, contributing to that feeling of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:19.080
overwhelmingly bright light in the darkness.
This is called a bloom effect, and at its heart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:24.360
it can be both a simple and surprisingly&nbsp;
complex effect to implement. Do it right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:29.520
and it looks subtle and sells that idea of an&nbsp;
overwhelmingly bright light, but do it wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.960
and the scene ends up looking a bit wonky,&nbsp;
kinda straight out of the mid 2000’s.
&nbsp;

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:39.900
Let’s talk about how it used to be done, way&nbsp;
back in the day. There’s really no official&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:45.660
“correct” way to do this, whatever works, but the&nbsp;
premise basically works like this. You’ve got a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:51.000
copy of your main framebuffer, and you’ll apply&nbsp;
some basic operations. You threshold the image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:57.480
meaning you choose the bright parts to bloom out.&nbsp;
You downsample it, mostly for performance reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.080
And you apply a bunch of blur to it, that’s&nbsp;
how you get that bleed over effect.
&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:07.140
Once you’ve done all that, they can be combined in&nbsp;
some handwavy way that looks nice to end up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:13.020
a bloomed out image. That’s the theory anyway.&nbsp;
It’s not really set in stone, this is kinda an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:17.760
artistic effect, so in reality you’re going to&nbsp;
just go with whatever works and looks nice.
&nbsp;

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:23.340
Let’s talk about thresholding an image first.&nbsp;
So what most people do is take the image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:28.620
and do some sort of luminance cutoff. The&nbsp;
reasoning behind this was simple. A lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:33.780
stuff only ran with 8-bit per channel. Nowadays&nbsp;
modern hardware is fast enough to support floating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:38.400
point buffers wherever you need them, but back&nbsp;
in the dark ages, you often went with 8 bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:44.760
channels. Xbox 360 had a 10 bit float format,&nbsp;
but I don’t think ps3 had an equivalent.
&nbsp;

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:50.040
So yeah, you take your image and there’s a bunch&nbsp;
of ways people did this. You could contrast the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.940
heck out of your image, basically preserving&nbsp;
the bright areas and dropping off the rest.
&nbsp;

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:58.320
You can also just set a luminance&nbsp;
threshold, so calculate luminance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.280
per-pixel and then drop off towards 0&nbsp;
everything below a certain threshold.
&nbsp;

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:07.980
The general idea is that you need to isolate some&nbsp;
part of the image for blooming, not all of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:12.120
because we’re going to be additively blending&nbsp;
this bad boy back on the screen later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:16.260
and without dropping off some parts,&nbsp;
it’ll look like a bit of a disaster.
&nbsp;

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:21.420
You can use the full size main colour target&nbsp;
directly, but working with a smaller version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:25.680
of that is faster and cheaper. So it’s&nbsp;
normal to take your main colour target,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:31.380
and down sample that to a fraction of the size.
Every time you halve your resolution, the # of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:38.760
pixels processed gets cut by 75%, which is pretty&nbsp;
insane savings. The tradeoff here is that, as you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.900
progressively downsample, you risk shimmering&nbsp;
artefacts due to the lack of resolution.
&nbsp;

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:49.380
Then there’s a blur pass, so you’ll take your main&nbsp;
colour, which has probably been downsampled, then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:56.640
you’ll blur it somehow. Now, a dead simple way is&nbsp;
good ol’ box blur, nothing beats that. The basic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.320
idea is to convolve the image with an equal weight&nbsp;
kernel, that might look something like this.
&nbsp;

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:07.920
And that basically amounts to, if you were to&nbsp;
zoom in and look at individual pixels, for any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:13.260
given pixel in the input image, you’d sample the&nbsp;
nearby pixels and then average them all together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:19.260
to produce the final pixel in the output image.
Go and do that a few zillion times for every pixel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:24.660
in the image, and you’ll get a slightly blurry&nbsp;
image. This has some obvious limitations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:30.300
For starters, to get a bigger radius, you can&nbsp;
either skip pixels, which just kinda looks bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:36.420
you can see the artefacts there pretty clearly
Or you can expand the kernel, which looks better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:41.880
but comes at the steep cost of being N^2 for the&nbsp;
radius you wanna blur. Bad options all around.
&nbsp;

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:48.840
Luckily, the box filter is what’s referred&nbsp;
to as a separable filter in image processing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:53.760
meaning that the 2d version can be&nbsp;
expressed as 2 1-dimensional filters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:58.380
That is, it can be done along the&nbsp;
horizontal axis, or the x axis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:03.900
and then the output of that can be run through&nbsp;
another filter on the vertical or y axis.
&nbsp;

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:10.320
So I can start with the source image, and we can&nbsp;
start by applying a box filter along the x-axis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:13.680
Notice the blurring in the&nbsp;
image is strictly horizontal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:19.080
Once we’ve finished doing that, we can apply a&nbsp;
2nd box filter along the y-axis, giving us the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:25.680
box filter in 2n steps rather than N^2.
An arguably higher quality blur is to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:29.400
a gaussian, which is just changing&nbsp;
the weights around in the kernel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:33.000
Instead of equal weights, you can bias&nbsp;
the weight towards the centre pixel.
&nbsp;

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.880
The kernel basically looks like this, kind of&nbsp;
a hill with raised centre and a gentle falloff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:45.000
towards the sides. Contrast that to a box filter,&nbsp;
which is exactly what it says, it’s a box.
&nbsp;

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:52.140
This is separable, just like the box blur, so it’s&nbsp;
easy to get going. There’s other kernels here too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:55.980
but I feel like the important takeaway&nbsp;
here is the general idea that you wanna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:59.700
make a blurry version of the scene.
And now that you’ve got your blurred out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:04.440
copy of the scene, you blend it back over the&nbsp;
screen as a post effect, generally an additive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:10.440
pass. And that’s it, that’s all it is.
Problem is, doing it this way is part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:15.420
the reason why the games in the mid 2000’s looked&nbsp;
so bloom heavy. They relied on using things like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:20.940
thresholds to bloom out. If you’ve got an 8 bit&nbsp;
per channel buffer, that means that the maximum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:27.720
value is 255. If you choose to say, use luminance&nbsp;
to threshold what’s bright and what isn’t, ie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:33.120
what blooms and what doesn’t. That approach makes&nbsp;
zero distinction between just a white object,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:38.520
and a glowing hot one. The end result being,&nbsp;
it's super hard to get under control.
&nbsp;

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:45.420
Back in the day, we developers thought it looked&nbsp;
awesome and soooo next-gen, and it kinda did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:50.580
so how about shut up. But let’s look at how&nbsp;
they’ve improved the approach in some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:56.220
major engines since those bright, bloomy days.
So, in general, any current generation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:59.640
implementation of bloom is going to be&nbsp;
trying to accomplish a few things.
&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:06.000
High quality, nuanced. We’re long past&nbsp;
the days where bloom is new and exciting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.740
so bloom implementations want to give that&nbsp;
feeling of an overwhelmingly bright light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:15.900
without beating over the head with it.
Performant: This should be obvious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:20.940
it should be fast, realllll fast.
Kawase’s Blur Filter
&nbsp;

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:28.920
Let’s talk about new approaches, but before that,&nbsp;
way back at GDC 2003, this was I think before I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:35.520
even started in the industry, there was&nbsp;
a presentation by Masaki Kawase, Kawacy?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:41.340
Uh whatever, it was a very clever way to use gpu&nbsp;
hardware to get a really nice blur for cheap.
&nbsp;

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:47.880
The idea is, take the main image, take 4&nbsp;
bilinear samples, and use those to downsample&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:52.920
progressively to a smaller resolution, in the&nbsp;
talks I think they mentioned ¼ resolution.
&nbsp;

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:58.680
After that, you’ll ping pong between 2&nbsp;
textures, each time taking 4 samples at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:03.960
a time with an increasing offset. By iterating&nbsp;
this over and over again, you can get a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:10.320
good approximation to a large gaussian blur, for&nbsp;
really few texture samples. This is important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:14.700
because this algorithm serves as the backbone&nbsp;
of pretty much all the modern approaches.
&nbsp;

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:21.840
Let’s start by taking a look at Unity’s bloom&nbsp;
implementation. In 2014, the folks at Sledgehammer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:26.520
games, they were the ones behind the latest&nbsp;
(at the time) iteration of Call of Duty.
&nbsp;

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:30.660
So they outlined how their bloom&nbsp;
implementation worked. Their technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:35.580
was an evolution of the Kawase filter we&nbsp;
just outlined. The idea is as follows:
&nbsp;

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:41.220
You take your framebuffer, and there’s no&nbsp;
threshold step. With HDR commonplace now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:47.160
this isn’t needed. Now you downsample it to&nbsp;
create basically a mip chain. The default way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:51.540
is to take a bilinear sample right in the&nbsp;
middle of every 4 texels, and the Kawase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:57.900
method showed you could take 4 taps instead of 1.&nbsp;
They decided, screw it, we’re taking 13 taps.
&nbsp;

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:02.700
And it’s really neat what they do here. So&nbsp;
if you’re looking at this group of pixels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:07.980
and they’re going to take 9 samples total,&nbsp;
the upper left 4 will form a group, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:13.440
upper right will form another, and so on. Lastly,&nbsp;
there’ll be one group of 4 pixels in the middle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:18.420
and that puts a large bias towards those&nbsp;
centre pixels. The idea being that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:22.860
should help reduce pulsing artefacts.
These are all combined with a simple equation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:27.420
with half the weight being the centre 4, and the&nbsp;
rest of the 4 groups getting equal weight.
&nbsp;

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:31.620
One extra trick they added here is&nbsp;
that on the very first downsample,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:36.360
they take the Karis average based on luminance,&nbsp;
which helps preserve bright pixels.
&nbsp;

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:41.700
Next, they progressively upsample, starting&nbsp;
with the smallest downsampled image.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:47.100
Each step, they apply a tent filter to the&nbsp;
downsampled version and also sum in the previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:52.620
mip. I think. This is based on the slides.
So once you do that for a while, you’ll end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:56.520
up with a fully downsampled and then&nbsp;
re-upsampled image, which you then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:01.080
lerp with the main image to get a bloomed out&nbsp;
scene, and overall it looks pretty good.
&nbsp;

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:05.520
Notice that the scene isn’t too bloomed out&nbsp;
like in shots from earlier in the video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:11.520
the bloom is more subtle and comes off&nbsp;
more as a glow rather than a weird haze.
&nbsp;

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:16.620
So unreal’s approach seems to be mostly an&nbsp;
extension on those same older techniques.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:22.320
They go over the general approach way back in&nbsp;
their Siggraph 2012 presentation on the technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:28.200
behind the “Unreal Engine 4 Elemental Demo”.
From what I can gather, they take the main image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:32.040
the framebuffer, and from that they&nbsp;
progressively downsample a fixed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:37.860
number of times using a plain Kawase blur.
They then do a similar upscaling process as Unity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:42.660
but from the sounds of it, they do a small&nbsp;
separable gaussian blur at each level, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:48.180
is artist configurable. So you can modify the&nbsp;
size of the blur at each level to your taste.
&nbsp;

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:52.860
They also add some knobs and dials for doing&nbsp;
things like applying tints to each level, giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:57.960
you that extra level of artistic control.
So I could, for example, specifically choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:02.400
to tint an individual layer of the bloom chain,&nbsp;
while leaving the rest as varying shades of grey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:08.280
or something to that effect. I haven’t tried&nbsp;
profiling it, my guess is that it’s slightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:12.540
more expensive than Unity’s approach but can&nbsp;
give higher quality and more flexible results.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:18.840
This is based on no actual research on my part.
So while most of these approaches are just tweaks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:23.580
and evolutions on Kawase’s approach from&nbsp;
way back, the newer versions of Unreal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:27.660
engine come with a fancy new convolution&nbsp;
bloom, which is really interesting.
&nbsp;

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:32.760
If you have a signal, let’s represent it&nbsp;
with a seemingly random function here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:38.580
then you can use something called the Fourier&nbsp;
transform to decompose that function into a series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:43.620
of sinusoids, a series of sin and cosines&nbsp;
of varying amplitudes and frequencies.
&nbsp;

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:49.320
If you take an image and imagine each&nbsp;
pixel as data points of a 2d signal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:55.680
of a 2 dimensional function, then we can use a&nbsp;
2D transform to convert this to frequency space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:01.080
and then we can use the inverse fourier transform&nbsp;
to bring this back to our original image.
&nbsp;

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:06.240
The whole theory and background behind the&nbsp;
fourier transform deserves a long video by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:11.760
itself, which I clearly didn't do. There&nbsp;
are some phenomenal resources on Youtube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:17.340
which I’ve got linked in the description. For&nbsp;
our purposes, I think it’s probably sufficient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:21.480
to know that this transformation exists and&nbsp;
what it buys you, in terms of functionality.
&nbsp;

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:25.320
Once you’ve converted this to the&nbsp;
frequency domain, there’s some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:30.120
really cool manipulations you can do.
A high pass filter is one that preserves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:35.940
high frequency information while removing low&nbsp;
frequency information, and that’s as simple as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:40.440
multiplying by zero’ing out the image near&nbsp;
the origin before doing the inverse FFT.
&nbsp;

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:46.320
A low pass filter is the exact opposite,&nbsp;
preserving low frequency information while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:51.840
discarding high, which is the inverse of what we&nbsp;
just did, and that gives us a really blurry image.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:58.080
In fact, I can actually perform a gaussian blur&nbsp;
in a similar way, by using the fourier transform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:04.860
of a gaussian filter, which lets me perform a&nbsp;
gaussian blur for pretty much free. I mean, free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.860 --> 00:12:10.620
after the FFT, which wasn’t even slightly free.
That kind of leads us to an interesting property&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.620 --> 00:12:16.560
of the frequency domain, multiplying 2 images&nbsp;
together in the frequency domain is the equivalent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:21.120
to convolving them in the time domain.
This is where Unreal’s new convolution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:25.260
bloom is so neat and different. You&nbsp;
can create custom bloom kernel shapes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:30.840
so here for example I can create a star like&nbsp;
object which I’ve done just by drawing some 2d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:37.560
sdf’s. I’ve also added a bit of a gaussian blurs&nbsp;
on top of that. Now, we can transform that kernel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:42.900
that I just made into frequency space via the&nbsp;
FFT, and multiple our fourier transformed image
&nbsp;

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:47.160
Then when we take the inverse&nbsp;
of that using the inverse FFT,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:53.160
that ends up being our blurry bloom buffer. When&nbsp;
we combine that with the framebuffer, it can give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:57.420
us this neat streaking effect that would be&nbsp;
a lot more difficult to do with conventional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.420 --> 00:13:03.180
methods but trivial using this approach.
The unfortunate downside is that doing an FFT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:09.360
is a heavy operation, but I imagine it’s one that&nbsp;
we’ll probably see more often as graphics hardware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:15.660
undoubtedly gets more and more powerful. There was&nbsp;
a time when doing simple loops was unfathomable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:20.220
so a few more iterations on hardware and&nbsp;
this will be a small blip on the profiler.
&nbsp;

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:22.440
Until next time,
Cheers

